---
import Todo from '@/components/ui/Todo.astro';
import ArticleLayout from '@/layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';

const published = new Date('2026-10-04');
const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Why do extension springs break at the hook?',
    a: 'The hook is a tight bend. Stress there is higher than in the body. The hook usually fails first. Treat body stress as a lower bound.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What hook types can you form?',
    a: 'Machine loops and extended hooks are the usual request. Other eyes and offsets we will confirm from your drawing.',
  },
];
---

<ArticleLayout
  route="extensionEnds"
  title="Extension Spring Ends"
  description="Extension spring hooks and loops: machine hook, extended hook, crossover and side. Why the hook fails first. What we usually form."
  h1="Extension spring end types"
  lede="The hook takes the load and usually fails first. Name the type, the orientation, and the length inside the hooks."
  published={published}
  context="an extension spring"
  takeaways={[
    'Machine loops and extended hooks are the usual request in this shop.',
    'Hook stress is higher than body stress.',
    'Give length inside hooks and whether the openings face the same way.',
  ]}
  toc={[
    { id: 'types', text: 'Common ends' },
    { id: 'fail', text: 'Why the hook fails' },
    { id: 'drawing', text: 'What to specify' },
    { id: 'questions', text: 'Questions' },
  ]}
  faqs={faqs}
  related={[
    { to: 'extension', label: 'Tension / extension springs', description: 'What we make.' },
    {
      to: 'extensionCalc',
      label: 'Extension spring calculator',
      description: 'Rate and body stress.',
    },
    {
      to: 'glossary',
      label: 'Spring glossary',
      description: 'Initial tension, hook stress, body coils.',
    },
  ]}
>
  <h2 id="types">Common ends</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Machine hook (machine loop).</strong> A simple loop formed on the last coil. The usual,
    cheapest end. Fine when the attachment is a pin on the spring axis.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Extended hook.</strong> The loop stands out on a short straight. Use it when the
    attachment is a little away from the body.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Crossover hook.</strong> The opening sits across the coil so the pull stays on the axis.{' '}
    <strong>Side hook.</strong> The opening sits to the side, for an offset attachment.
  </p>
  <p>
    Machine loops and extended hooks are the usual request here. Other eyes and offsets we confirm
    from the drawing.
    <Todo text="hook types actually formed on this floor." />
  </p>

  <h2 id="fail">Why the hook fails first</h2>
  <p>
    The hook is bent to a tighter radius than the body. Stress there is higher. A calculator that
    only reports body shear stress is a lower bound — see the{' '}
    <a href="/tools/extension-spring-calculator/">extension spring calculator</a>. Reduce hook
    stress with a larger bend radius or a crossover if the drawing allows it.
  </p>

  <h2 id="drawing">What to specify</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Hook type at each end (they can differ)</li>
    <li>Orientation: openings in line, at 90°, or opposite</li>
    <li>
      <a href="/resources/spring-glossary/#length-inside-hooks">Length inside hooks</a>
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href="/resources/spring-glossary/#initial-tension">Initial tension</a> if the hook must not
      rattle
    </li>
  </ul>
</ArticleLayout>
